      "math_academy_id": "q-132159",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-1,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X<-3.1)=0.3632,$ find $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ | $\\,0.2912\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3336\\,$ | $\\,0.3300\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "First,  we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-3.1) & =0.3632 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{-3.1-(-1)}{\\sigma }) & =0.3632 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{2.1}{\\sigma }) & =0.3632 \\\\ -\\frac{2.1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.3632)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.35)=0.3632\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.3632)=-0.35.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2.1}{\\sigma } & =-0.35 \\\\ \\sigma  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
